📊 ANAPLAN TO POWER BI: Direct Data Integration Guide
  - This guide explains how to set up a secure, automated pipeline from Anaplan to Power BI, leveraging Anaplan's REST API, 
    OAuth 2.0, and Power BI’s custom connector or gateway setup.

🧭 1. Architecture Overview
  Anaplan Model (Saved View) 
      ⬇
  Anaplan Export Action (CSV or JSON)
      ⬇
  Anaplan REST API (OAuth 2.0 Token Auth)
      ⬇
  Power BI (via Power Query M Script or Custom Connector)

  - Format Supported: CSV (recommended), JSON
  - Integration Style: API-based Pull (preferred over manual export)
  - Security: OAuth 2.0 (Client Credentials)

🧰 2. Prerequisites
  - Anaplan Workspace Admin privileges
  - Anaplan OAuth 2.0 credentials (see OAUTH2_SETUP_GUIDE.md)
  - Created and tested Export Actions in Anaplan
  - Power BI Desktop (latest version)
  -- Optional: Power BI Gateway for scheduled refreshes

🛠️ 3. Anaplan Side Configuration
  ✅ A. Create a Saved View & Export Action
    - In your target model, create a Saved View with filtered data (e.g., EXP_Actuals_YTD)
      Go to Actions > Export → Create New Export:
      1) Format: CSV
      2) Label: Export_Actuals_YTD
      3) Save and test the action

  ✅ B. Get Export Metadata
    Use Anaplan API to retrieve:
    - Workspace ID
    - Model ID
    - Export ID

  You can use:
  > GET https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/workspaces
  > GET https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/workspaces/{workspaceId}/models
  > GET https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/models/{modelId}/exports

⚙️ 4. Power BI Connection Methods
  🔗 A. Option 1: Power Query M Script (No Custom Connector)
  Use Web.Contents with OAuth2 headers to pull data directly: 
      let
          // Replace with your token fetch logic or static token (test only)
          AccessToken = "Bearer eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1Qi...",
          Source = Web.Contents(
              "https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/workspaces/{workspaceId}/models/{modelId}/exports/{exportId}/tasks",
              [
                  Headers = [
                      Authorization = AccessToken,
                      Accept = "application/json"
                  ]
              ]
          )
      in
          Source
  🔐 Tip: Use Power Automate or Azure Functions to retrieve fresh tokens and inject them into Power BI Service for scheduled refresh.
  
  🔧 B. Option 2: Build a Power BI Custom Connector
  If you're looking for a reusable and secured integration:
    - Use Power Query SDK in Visual Studio Code
    - Build connector with:
    - OAuth 2.0 token injection
    - Workspace/Model/Export dropdown selectors
    - Deploy via organizational Power BI Gateway

⏱️ 5. Automating the Export + Load
  Use Anaplan API to launch export action:
  > POST https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/models/{modelId}/exports/{exportId}/tasks
  
  Poll for completion:
  > GET https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/models/{modelId}/exports/{exportId}/tasks/{taskId}
  
  Download the file:
  > GET https://api.anaplan.com/2/0/models/{modelId}/exports/{exportId}/tasks/{taskId}/dump
  
  You can automate this flow using:
  - Python (requests + pandas) → write directly to .csv or SQL
  - Azure Data Factory → store in Blob → Power BI reads from Blob
  - AWS Lambda + S3 → push dump → Power BI fetches from S3

🔐 6. Security Considerations
  - Never expose client_secret in Power BI directly
  - Use Azure Key Vault or Power Automate Flow to secure token retrieval
  - Rotate secrets every 90 days
  - Ensure Anaplan export users have read-only access to the relevant models

🧪 7. Testing & Validation
  Test                | Method
  Can fetch token	    | Postman or Python
  Export Action works |	Via Anaplan GUI
  API pulls file	    | API GET dump response
  Power BI reads file	| via connector or Power Query

💡 8. Pro Tips
  - Keep your export actions static (no dropdowns, no UI prompts)
  - For large models, break exports into modular files by time or region
  - Consider Azure Blob or S3 as intermediate staging zones
  - Document and version your exports (e.g., Export_Forecast_Q2_2025)

